All feedback from API review (https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/36022#issuecomment-805134925) are also addressed:
- We established during reviews that unique ids per assembly wouldn't work. So, we changed the scope of uniqueness for the event IDs to be the class in which the log method is declared. It doesn't check up the inheritance hierarchy for conflicting IDs because it also wouldn't be possible cross-assembly boundaries. The diagnostic message for this condition would be a warning indicating the class name.
- When generating instance-mode logging methods, the field that holds the logger is now determined by looking at available fields. Generator produces diagnostic error if there isn't a field of the right type or if there is more than one such field. This also doesn't look up the inheritance hierarchy and does not look for properties, but it would be possible to add this capability in the future. (show sample)
- We also carefully considered the C# 10 string interpolation builder approach, I drafted a summary for that research investigation in this doc: https://github.com/dotnet/designs/pull/195. The consensus there is to keep the logging generator for the following top two reasons:
   - Wanting to enable the exact format syntax required by https://messagetemplates.org/
   - Wanting to be able to pass all of original data as-is without any complication around how it's stored prior to something being done with it other than creating a string

